# Verified non-secrets in the git history, ignored by fingerprint.
#
# Why this exists alongside .gitleaks.toml: the regex allowlist there is honoured
# by gitleaks 8.30 (what `brew install gitleaks` gives you) but not by 8.24.3,
# which is what gitleaks-action v3.0.0 pins. So a local `gitleaks git .` reports
# a clean history while CI reports three leaks from the same config. Fingerprints
# are respected by both, so they are the portable way to record a verdict.
#
# This only matters for the weekly scheduled run. Push runs scan just the new
# commits; the cron walks all of history, so without this it fails every week on
# the same three findings — which is how a real leak ends up buried in noise.
#
# A fingerprint is commit:path:rule:line, so each line pins one occurrence in one
# commit. A new secret in any of these files still fails the scan.

